.TH FLOWM 1 "July 2026" "flowm 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
flowm \- a floating, reparenting window manager for X11
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B flowm
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B flowm
is a small floating window manager.  Each top-level window is
reparented into a decorated frame with a border, a title bar and a
close button.  Windows are moved by dragging the title bar (or
Mod4+left-drag anywhere in the window) and resized with
Mod4+right-drag.  Up to 16 virtual workspaces are provided, together
with a built-in status bar showing the workspace list, the focused
window title and a clock.
.PP
.B flowm
implements the relevant parts of the ICCCM and EWMH specifications:
polite window closing via
.BR WM_DELETE_WINDOW ,
focus handover via
.BR WM_TAKE_FOCUS ,
size hints during resize, fullscreen via
.BR _NET_WM_STATE_FULLSCREEN ,
and the usual root window properties consumed by pagers and task bars.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " file"
Read configuration from
.I file
instead of the default location.
.TP
.B \-d
Enable debug logging to standard error.
.TP
.B \-t
Load the configuration, report every error with its line number and exit.
The exit status is 1 if the file cannot be opened or contains errors.
No X server is needed.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is searched for at
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/flowm/flowm.conf
and then
.IR ~/.config/flowm/flowm.conf .
A missing file is not an error; built-in defaults cover everything.
The file consists of
.B key = value
lines,
.B bind = CHORD ACTION
key bindings and
.B rule = MATCH options
per-application rules.
.B mode_bind = MODE CHORD ACTION
defines a binding active only in key mode MODE, entered with the
.B mode
action.
.B unbind = CHORD
removes a default binding and
.B include = FILE
reads another file relative to the including one.  See the shipped
.I flowm.conf
for the complete, commented list of options.
.SH DEFAULT BINDINGS
Mod4 is the Super ("Windows") key.
.TP
.B Mod4+Return
Spawn a terminal
.RB ( xterm ).
.TP
.B Mod4+d
Spawn
.BR dmenu_run .
.TP
.B Mod4+q / Mod4+Shift+q
Close politely / kill the focused window.
.TP
.B Mod4+f, Mod4+m, Mod4+c, Mod4+s
Fullscreen, maximize, center, toggle sticky.
.TP
.B Mod4+Control+Left / Right / Up / Down
Snap focused window to the left / right / top / bottom half of the usable
area.  Pressing the same chord again restores the previous geometry.
The actions
.BR snap_top_left ,
.BR snap_top_right ,
.B snap_bottom_left
and
.B snap_bottom_right
snap to a quarter and have no default binding.
.TP
.B Mod4+BackSpace
Focus the previously focused window.
.TP
.B Mod4+grave
Scratchpad.  The first use hides the focused window and makes it the
scratchpad window.  Later uses show it on the current workspace or hide it.
.TP
.B Mod4+Tab / Mod4+Shift+Tab
Cycle focus forward / backward.
.TP
.B Mod4+1..9 / Mod4+Shift+1..9
Switch to / send window to workspace.
.TP
.B Mod4+Shift+e
Quit
.BR flowm .
.SH IPC
.B utils/panelctl
writes commands to the
.B _FLOWM_CMD
property of the root window.  Lines of the form
.B key=value
change a configuration option.
.B reload
reloads the configuration file.
.B get workspace
,
.B get focused
and
.B get windows
store a tab separated answer in the
.B _FLOWM_REPLY
root window property, which
.B panelctl get
prints.
.PP
Text set with
.BR xsetroot " " \-name
is shown in the status bar.
.PP
Windows of type dock that set
.B _NET_WM_STRUT_PARTIAL
or
.B _NET_WM_STRUT
reduce the area used for maximizing and snapping.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the configuration file in place: rebind keys, re-apply colors,
fonts, workspace names and rules to the running session without
restarting
.BR flowm ,
so managed windows keep their positions.
.TP
.B SIGTERM, SIGINT
Quit
.BR flowm .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
The X display to manage.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for the configuration file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/flowm/flowm.conf
Per-user configuration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR X (7),
.BR xterm (1),
.BR dmenu (1)
.SH BUGS
Multi-monitor layouts are treated as one large screen (no RandR
awareness); this is a deliberate scope limit to keep libX11 the only
dependency.
